          They show how a trespasse is worked in practice: valuation, negotiation, triangulation with the landlord, timing.
          Companion to the <a href="/en/trespasse-guide" style="color:var(--g-accent);font-weight:600;">guide</a> and the <a href="/en/trespasse-faq" style="color:var(--g-accent);font-weight:600;">FAQ</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="caso1">Case 1 — Hair salon in Amoreiras</h2>

        <div class="hl">
          <p>
            <strong>Sector:</strong> wellness services · <strong>Area:</strong> Amoreiras / Marquês de Pombal · <strong>Positioning:</strong> urban proximity clientele, mixed office/residential flow · <strong>Status:</strong> active mandate
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Context</h3>
        <p>
          Hair salon established for several years, loyal clientele, located on a street with mixed office/residential flow. Small team (3 stations). Steady revenue, typical sector margins (25–35% EBITDA). The seller, nearing retirement, is looking to pass the torch — not sell to the highest bidder, but ensure the salon's continuity.
        </p>

        <h3>The challenge</h3>
        <p>
          Two parallel challenges. <strong>Fair valuation</strong> of a proximity service, which isn't valued like a restaurant: the going concern is worth essentially the recurring clientele, the lease contract (historically favourable rent), and equipment condition. <strong>Finding the right profile</strong> — not an investor buying to flip, but an operator or small group who will maintain the salon's identity and team.
        </p>

        <h3>TurnKey's approach</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Valuation anchored on the <strong>3-year average EBITDA</strong>, adjusted for the favourable residual rent (key asset) and equipment depreciation</li>
          <li>Blind teaser sent to the internal buyer base: explicit exclusion of pure investors, focus on operators seeking a first or second unit</li>
          <li>In-depth prior qualification of each candidate (3 years of accounts, operating plan, ability to preserve the team)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Outcome</h3>
        <p>
          Deal currently in qualification phase with 2 serious candidates. Expected timing: signing before year-end. Typical timeline for a proximity service of this type: 60 to 90 days between mandate and signing.
        </p>

        <div class="hl">
          <p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> for proximity services (salon, studio, small retail), value lies not in revenue but in <strong>clientele loyalty</strong> and <strong>the lease</strong>. Finding the right buyer (profile, project, equity) matters more than the highest price — because a poorly prepared handover destroys the going concern in 6 months.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="caso2">Case 2 — Café-restaurant in an upscale residential area</h2>

        <div class="hl">
          <p>
            <strong>Sector:</strong> F&amp;B · <strong>Area:</strong> upscale residential axis (Lapa / Estrela) · <strong>Positioning:</strong> neighbourhood establishment, loyal local clientele · <strong>Status:</strong> negotiation in final phase
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Context</h3>
        <p>
          Café-restaurant open for several years on a premium residential axis in Lisbon. Three registered fractions (two shops and a basement) with an outdoor terrace. The seller decided to close the operation to shift professional direction. A buyer offer already identified: a neighbouring hotel group interested in adding an F&amp;B extension to its operation.
        </p>

        <h3>The challenge: seller / landlord / buyer triangulation</h3>
        <p>
          A textbook three-party case. The <strong>landlord</strong> uses the handover to demand a rent significantly higher than before — initial ask at <strong>€6,000/month</strong>. The <strong>buyer</strong> sets a ceiling at <strong>€3,800/month</strong> for their numbers to work. In between: <strong>a €2,200/month gap</strong> that will blow up the deal unless a meeting point is found.
        </p>

        <p>
          Compounding issue: an initial valuation commissioned by the landlord from an external agency inflated the leased surface (counted the terrace as part of the leased unit, which it isn't formally). Result: an artificially high reference value anchoring the entire discussion on false ground.
        </p>

        <h3>TurnKey's approach</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Technical challenge to the valuation:</strong> presentation of the official fraction titles, demonstration that the terrace falls outside the leased perimeter, recalibration of the market reference</li>
          <li><strong>Cross-negotiation of rent / trespasse price:</strong> proposed to absorb part of the gap (€200/month) via an equivalent reduction in the trespasse price, so each party finds its own equation</li>
          <li><strong>Lease clause review:</strong> identified clause 7.8 placing structural maintenance works on the landlord — leverage used in the broader negotiation balance</li>
          <li><strong>Confidential marketing:</strong> blind teaser under code name, in pt-PT, sent to other qualified candidates (F&amp;B groups, wellness operators) to maintain healthy competitive pressure</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Outcome</h3>
        <p>
          The final negotiation phase is still underway at the time of writing. The operation was closed within the seller's planned timeline (independent of the signing date) to avoid running costs without revenue. Final target price being anchored in an intermediate zone between both parties' expectations, with a rent in agreement territory for all three.
        </p>

        <div class="hl">
          <p>
            <strong>Takeaway:</strong> a trespasse isn't a two-party deal but <strong>often a three-party one</strong> (seller, buyer, landlord). The balance point only exists if all three equations find their answer. That requires: <strong>challenging</strong> an artificially high valuation (an external agency isn't an infallible judge), <strong>cross-negotiating</strong> the moving parts (trespasse price ↔ rent ↔ lease duration), and <strong>decoupling</strong> the operational calendar (closure) from the contractual one (signing) so the seller doesn't bear sunk costs during negotiations.
          </p>
        </div>

